# 
# this is some sage code i wrote to illustrate the use of rsa 
# on Tuesday, Feb. 5, 2013. 
# 
# Please feel free to modify and add to the code. 
# This initial version merely takes Chandler's 
# ``the big sleep'' as plain-text, converts it to 
# to a list of ascii codes, converts that to a list 
# of numbers in Z/26Z, takes bunches of 97 to create 
# a base-26 integer, adds 2 mod n (for a rather large n), 
# then applies the encryption exponent e to get a new 
# list of large numbers. 
# the code ends by taking one of the large numbers and 
# converting it back to plain-text just for fun. 
#
# the code is certainly primitive, and is simply meant to 
# introduce a bit of the syntax of sage. 
# 
# more comments will be introduced later...

# this is the full text of raymond t. chandler's ``the big sleep''...
plain=''

nums=map(ord,list(plain)) 
numchars=len(nums)

# got these primes from http://primes.utm.edu/lists/small/
# both have about 130 digits 
p=49105133753678962419391374496727457097027115514864975959094375339860082156189031654878635228598019107441653554142227931036658923353143622433
q=64245974067698974727308576291067189905635129662015537917175582385219809100094736540969262037560193877488366836626125921856719528308413429711

# i could have picked smaller p and q: all i need is an 
# n bigger than 138 digits
n=p*q 
phin=(p-1)*(q-1)

e=9872398472439
d=1/e % phin 

k=0
newnums=[0 for i in range(numchars/97)]
for i in range(0,numchars,97): 
 newnums[k]=0 
 for j in range(0,97): 
  newnums[k]=newnums[k]+(nums[i+j]-97)*26^j
 
 newnums[k]=Mod(newnums[k]+2,n) 
 k=k+1 

print(nums)
print(newnums)
print(numchars) 

ciph=[0 for i in range(numchars/97)]
for i in range(numchars/97):  
 ciph[i]=newnums[i]^e  

test=Integer(ciph[0]-2)
prenums=test.digits(26)
for i in range(198): 
 prenums[i]=prenums[i]+97 
ciphertext=map(chr,prenums)

print(join(ciphertext)) 


